#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054470 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de7d614-76c9-5133-a4df-95f079467cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality With No Constant Term Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/id #uuid "b4b933f0-f414-5681-a466-0f070c903a41" :question/math-academy-id "e-5174" :question/problem "Find the solution of $x^2-3x \\lt 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} - 3x & < 0 \\\\ x(x - 3) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x-3)=0$. The roots are $x=0$ and $x=3$, and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty,0)$, $(0,3)$, and $(3,\\infty)$.\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x(x-3)$ | $({\\color{red}-})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{blue}+}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{red}-}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{blue}+})={\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x-3) \\lt 0$, we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x-3)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(0, 3)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x^2-3x \\lt 0$ for all $x\\in (0,3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054471 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/id #uuid "79c8bc06-0c46-51d6-89fe-daa3a6832247" :question/math-academy-id "e-5175" :question/problem "Find the solution of $x^2-3x +2\\geq 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} - 3x + 2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x - 1)(x - 2) & \\ge 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x-2)=0\n$$\n\nThe roots are $x = 1$ and $x=2$, and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty,1)$, $(1,2)$, and $(2,\\infty)$.\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $(1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x-1)(x-2)$ | $({\\color{red}-})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{blue}+}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{red}-}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{blue}+})={\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x-2) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-1)(x-2)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty, 1)$ and $(2,\\infty)$. Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=1$ and $x=2$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x-2) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $[MATH: x\\in (-\\infty, 1] \\cup [2, \\infty).]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054472 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/id #uuid "e73ff639-68a7-57b0-93af-05567e2bc44f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5176" :question/problem "Find the solution of $x^2+x \\leq 6$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + x & \\le 6 \\\\ x^{2} + x - 6 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x - 2)(x + 3) & \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+3) = 0\n$$\n\nThe roots are $x=2$ and $x=-3$, and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty, -3)$, $(-3,2)$, and $(2,\\infty)$.\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $(-3,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x+3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x+3)(x-2)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+3) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nwe look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-2)(x+3)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-3,2)$. Also, we know that the expression equals zero at the points $x=-3$ and $x=2$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+3) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in[-3,2]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054473 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b23c4da-7a8d-5076-a4e9-8bcde40bc088" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table of Signs"}] :question/id #uuid "fe73a302-d48f-504f-8b8d-553f4ecd9237" :question/math-academy-id "e-5191" :question/problem "Complete the following sign table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ |  |  |  |\n| $x+1$ |  |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We need to fill in each box with the sign of the corresponding expression on the corresponding interval.\n\nLet's start down the first column. In the interval $(-\\infty, -1)$, we choose a test point $x_1=-2$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2 &= -2 - 2 =-4 < 0, \\\\\nx + 1 &= -2 + 1 =-1 < 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso both the expressions $x-2$ and $x+1$ are negative $({\\color{red}-})$ on the interval $(-\\infty,-1)$.\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ |  |  |  |\n\nTo find the sign of $(x-2)(x+1)$ on this interval, we can just multiply the corresponding signs of $x-2$ and $x+1$:\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-}) \\times ({\\color{red}-}) = ({\\color{blue}+})\n$$\n\nSo $(x-2)(x+1)$ is positive $({\\color{blue}+})$ on this interval, and we can complete the first column of the table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |  |  |\n\nTo find the entries in the other columns, we use the same procedure.\n\n- In the interval $(-1,2)$, we can choose a test point $x_2=0$. Then we have\n$x - 2|= 0 - 2 = (-),; x + 1|= 0 + 1 = (+),; (x - 2)(x + 1)|= (-) \\cdot (+) = (-)$,\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |\n\n- In the interval $(2,\\infty)$, we can choose a test point $x_3=3$. Then we have\n$x - 2|= 3 - 2 = (+),; x + 1|= 3 + 1 = (+),; (x - 2)(x + 1)|= (+) \\cdot (+) = (+)$,\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "848342fc-9f75-506c-9e1f-84bc60fa69cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-2, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052302} {:answer/id #uuid "bee97ef2-4772-5ede-b714-55ad3fd3439d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-5, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052303} {:answer/id #uuid "f8cd13eb-3e34-5dde-a8c2-7ed0561e2380" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-2)∪(5, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052304} {:answer/id #uuid "2f849b33-75f3-5ec2-8c41-fe14d62df017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞, 2)∪(5, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052305} {:answer/id #uuid "36944fd7-c1f8-5a37-a131-1f943cf713b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-5)∪(2, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8cd13eb-3e34-5dde-a8c2-7ed0561e2380" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-2)∪(5, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052304} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0e024fc-3dc1-54a6-9f92-bc0201656bee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052301}] :question/id #uuid "7824abae-ac0a-561f-a7e4-242996de64c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-27068" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is $x^{2} - 3x - 10 > 0$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fdb5392-4c1b-5454-b0ac-de625db4f374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ [- 4, 3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052314